      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1766643003571,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Final Template: \n\n\n1. Requirements: \n    -> understand crystal clear\n    -> understand flow wise or image like if this is software \n        what would be happening and what method or api , that particular flow\n        will be calling. \n    -> if not able to understand ask doubts \n    -> once we understood question properly , jump to step 2. \n    -> how to read the requirment so that half of your implementation sorted.\n        check step 2. \n \n\n2. How to write code. \n    -> While reading the requirements, find out the entities from that.\n    -> this is bit hard, but would be able to click by practice or multiple problem \n        solving. \n    -> extract out the entities on high level ( check hld -> db schema part ) \n    -> once we have entities/models. \n\n    -> while reading if you have change better to write entities/model in ide itself to\n        save your time. \n\n    -> How to write entities/models as code ? \n            -> Once we have entities:\n            -> write those entities\n            -> find relation between them.\n            -> 1:1\n                -> write 1:1 class in both entity.\n                -> or if you think require on one side, then write on \n                    on entity, its flexible as per implementation.\n                -> mostly we write any of side.\n            -> 1:m\n                -> again can write both side.\n                -> one can be list of , other side only one.\n                -> in this case some time not require on 1 side, then not write.\n                -> mostly we write like database on , 1 size as single not list.\n            -> m:m\n                -> here like db schema we need not to create another entity\n                    for between of both entity , \n                -> instead write list of on both size, or any of the \n                    size as per the implementation requirements.\n\n    -> once we do this write the model method or can also write later on as per implmentation\n        or requirements.\n    -> also think the design pattern here as well , like creational , structural , stratedy etc behaviour.\n    -> see do we have diferent types of that entity \n        -> then create interface or abstract class and write multiple implementations. \n\n\n    -> once we write all these in models directory. ( 20 min on model creation )\n\n    -> its time to create the repository layer and service layer creation.\n    -> as of now ignore controller layer-> since it will take time, if get time can be \n        done later one, as of now work with main. \n        \n        controller -> layerr basically send data to service + status / handling. \n        pass dto  to service -> service change to model -> repository. \n\n    -> How to write code after model ? \n\n        -> complete one flow -> one service -> one repo -> run this. \n            -> write repo, write inmemory map, getter, setter etc method.\n                 -> can take reference from entity, basically its a dao of entities.\n                     like userDao, accountDao etc. and write methods here.\n            -> create dao user map -> map<int,user> , something like this. \n            -> write service layer, add repo instance, write actual methods asked in requirements.\n                -> write business logic , play with repo for crud of data , \n                -> here also we can take help form entities\n                    -> usually have like userservice, account service. etc.\n                    -> make sure to do the one first which method require us.\n                    -> if not user related requirements , just creat repo\n                    -> no need to create service , then create user with service.\n                -> write code to solve problem.\n                -> complete code.\n        -> code flow by flow.\n        -> there can be multiple repo and multple service.\n             -> since time is limited try to reduce these.\n             -> some time if time given < 90, then no event write repository. \n             -> write repo code in service as per our problem usecase.\n\n    -> when we do class diagram ( without coding ) \n         -> we do actually write the model name.\n         -> their attributes as per above models layer.\n         -> in methods we write  , which we are writing in service. \n\n\n\n\n3. Review part / Bonus part: \n    -> complete code before timing like 30 mins. \n    -> see later on if we can add more design patter.\n    -> test code\n    -> restructure\n    -> work on bonus, but make sure not do regression. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Final Template: \n\n\n1. Requirements: \n    -> understand crystal clear\n    -> understand flow wise or image like if this is software \n        what would be happening and what method or api , that particular flow\n        will be calling. \n    -> if not able to understand ask doubts \n    -> once we understood question properly , jump to step 2. \n    -> how to read the requirment so that half of your implementation sorted.\n        check step 2. \n \n\n2. How to write code. \n    -> While reading the requirements, find out the entities from that.\n    -> this is bit hard, but would be able to click by practice or multiple problem \n        solving. \n    -> extract out the entities on high level ( check hld -> db schema part ) \n    -> once we have entities/models. \n\n    -> while reading if you have change better to write entities/model in ide itself to\n        save your time. \n\n    -> How to write entities/models as code ? \n            -> Once we have entities:\n            -> write those entities\n            -> find relation between them.\n            -> 1:1\n                -> write 1:1 class in both entity.\n                -> or if you think require on one side, then write on \n                    on entity, its flexible as per implementation.\n                -> mostly we write any of side.\n            -> 1:m\n                -> again can write both side.\n                -> one can be list of , other side only one.\n                -> in this case some time not require on 1 side, then not write.\n                -> mostly we write like database on , 1 size as single not list.\n            -> m:m\n                -> here like db schema we need not to create another entity\n                    for between of both entity , \n                -> instead write list of on both size, or any of the \n                    size as per the implementation requirements.\n\n    -> once we do this write the model method or can also write later on as per implmentation\n        or requirements.\n    -> also think the design pattern here as well , like creational , structural , stratedy etc behaviour.\n    -> see do we have diferent types of that entity \n        -> then create interface or abstract class and write multiple implementations. \n\n\n    -> once we write all these in models directory. ( 20 min on model creation )\n\n    -> its time to create the repository layer and service layer creation.\n    -> as of now ignore controller layer-> since it will take time, if get time can be \n        done later one, as of now work with main. \n        \n        controller -> layerr basically send data to service + status / handling. \n        pass dto  to service -> service change to model -> repository. \n\n    -> How to write code after model ? \n\n        -> complete one flow -> one service -> one repo -> run this. \n            -> write repo, write inmemory map, getter, setter etc method.\n                 -> can take reference from entity, basically its a dao of entities.\n                     like userDao, accountDao etc. and write methods here.\n            -> create dao user map -> map<int,user> , something like this. \n            -> write service layer, add repo instance, write actual methods asked in requirements.\n                -> write business logic , play with repo for crud of data , \n                -> here also we can take help form entities\n                    -> usually have like userservice, account service. etc.\n                    -> make sure to do the one first which method require us.\n                    -> if not user related requirements , just creat repo\n                    -> no need to create service , then create user with service.\n                -> write code to solve problem.\n                -> complete code.\n        -> code flow by flow.\n        -> there can be multiple repo and multple service.\n             -> since time is limited try to reduce these.\n             -> some time if time given < 90, then no event write repository. \n             -> write repo code in service as per our problem usecase.\n\n    -> when we do class diagram ( without coding ) \n         -> we do actually write the model name.\n         -> their attributes as per above models layer.\n         -> in methods we write  , which we are writing in service. \n\n\n\n\n3. Review part / Bonus part: \n    -> complete code before timing like 30 mins. \n    -> see later on if we can add more design patter.\n    -> test code\n    -> restructure\n    -> work on bonus, but make sure not do regression. ",
