      "text": "Let's learn with example ( flipkart machine coding 4+)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Let's learn with example ( flipkart machine coding 4+)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "UQ1bHTnQjuxbuceTNZt10",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1681.341532535078,
      "y": 627.336613703399,
      "width": 1142.5418375434094,
      "height": 2777.7557086016027,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aJ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1209270219,
      "version": 1457,
      "versionNonce": 995221067,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1766657920271,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Problem Details\n\nPayments are an integral component of any e-commerce or fintech system. With the advent of Digital India, multiple\npayment ecosystems have emerged, such as Payment Gateways, UPI, and the RuPay network. These systems are\neasy to integrate, allowing organizations to get started within days.\n\nWith the rise of multiple payment players, organizations often integrate more than one payment gateway and switch\nbetween them based on specific use cases.\n\nCustomers can choose different payment methods such as card, UPI, or net banking. Below is a general runbook for\nmaking a payment.\n\n\nPayment Flow\n -> Select a payment mode\n -> Enter payment details\n        -> Net Banking: username and password\n        -> Credit/Debit Card: card number, expiry date, CVV\n        -> UPI: VPA\n\n\n\nProblem Statement:\n\nImplement a Payment Gateway (PG) that facilitates payments for its clients. The following points should be\nconsidered during implementation.\n\nReal-World Example\n\nFlipkart integrates multiple payment gateways such as Razorpay, Citrus, PaySafe, and CCAvenue. These gateways\nare used based on different use cases. \n\nFor example, \nall credit card transactions may be routed to Razorpay, \nwhile net banking transactions are routed to CCAvenue.\n\nEach payment gateway internally integrates with multiple banks that actually process the payments.\n\nThe expectation is to build a payment gateway similar to Razorpay or CCAvenue that can onboard clients \n\nlike Flipkart and process transactions.\nClient: Flipkart (a PG can have multiple clients)\n\n\nPG: The system to be implemented\nBank: A PG can integrate with multiple banks; candidates may implement mock or full-fledged bank classes\nFeature Requirements\n -> The PG should support onboarding multiple clients\n -> The PG should support multiple bank integrations (e.g., HDFC, ICICI, SBI)\n -> The PG should support multiple payment methods such as UPI, Credit/Debit Card, and Net Banking\n -> The PG should route payments to specific banks based on defined criteria\n    -> Example: credit card payments go to HDFC, \n    -> net banking to ICICI\n-> The PG should support traffic distribution across banks\n    -> Example: 30% traffic to Bank A and 70% to Bank B\n-> Clients should be able to choose supported payment methods\n    -> Example: only UPI, or all except net banking\n\n\nAssumptions\n-> Banks may randomly return success or failure; this can be mocked using a random function\n-> Payments should be processed only if required parameters are provided\n    -> Net banking requires user ID and password\n    -> Card payments require card details\n    -> Banks require OTP verification after validating instrument details\n    -> For simplicity, OTP verification can be skipped\n\n\n\nCode Expectations\n-> All data should be maintained in memory\n-> Any programming language can be used\n-> Simple function-based entry points are expected\n-> No credit for REST APIs or framework-heavy implementations\n-> Emphasis should be on clean design and implementation rather than complex algorithms\n\n\n\nRequired Functions\n-> addClient(): Add a client to the PG -> clientservice -> clientmodel -> clientdao\n-> removeClient(): Remove a client from the PG. -> clientservice  clientdao\n-> hasClient(): Check whether a client exists in the PG -> \n-> listSupportedPaymodes(): Show payment modes supported by the PG\n    -> If a client is passed, show payment modes supported by that client\n-> addSupportForPaymode(): Add payment mode support to the PG\n    -> If a client is passed, add payment mode support for that client\n-> removePaymode(): Remove a payment mode from the PG or a specific client\n-> showDistribution(): Display the current traffic distribution across banks\n-> makePayment(): Process a payment using the provided payment details\n\n\n\nEvaluation Criteria\n\n-> Working implementation\n-> Code readability and clarity\n-> Proper use of OOP and Object-Oriented Design principles\n-> Clear separation of concerns\n-> Testability and TDD-oriented design\n-> Language proficiency\n-> Test cases (bonus points)\n\n\n\nExtension Problems\n-> Ability for the router to dynamically shift traffic based on bank success rates\n-> Ability to route traffic based on payment instruments, not just banks\n\n\n\nExecution Time Limit: 3 seconds (Java)",
      "fontSize": 20.019860962894434,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Problem Details\n\nPayments are an integral component of any e-commerce or fintech system. With the advent of Digital India, multiple payment ecosystems have emerged, such as Payment Gateways, UPI, and the RuPay network. These systems are easy to integrate, allowing organizations to get started within days.\n\nWith the rise of multiple payment players, organizations often integrate more than one payment gateway and switch between them based on specific use cases.\n\nCustomers can choose different payment methods such as card, UPI, or net banking. Below is a general runbook for making a payment.\n\n\nPayment Flow\n -> Select a payment mode\n -> Enter payment details\n        -> Net Banking: username and password\n        -> Credit/Debit Card: card number, expiry date, CVV\n        -> UPI: VPA\n\n\n\nProblem Statement:\n\nImplement a Payment Gateway (PG) that facilitates payments for its clients. The following points should be considered during implementation.\n\nReal-World Example\n\nFlipkart integrates multiple payment gateways such as Razorpay, Citrus, PaySafe, and CCAvenue. These gateways are used based on different use cases. \n\nFor example, \nall credit card transactions may be routed to Razorpay, \nwhile net banking transactions are routed to CCAvenue.\n\nEach payment gateway internally integrates with multiple banks that actually process the payments.\n\nThe expectation is to build a payment gateway similar to Razorpay or CCAvenue that can onboard clients \n\nlike Flipkart and process transactions.\nClient: Flipkart (a PG can have multiple clients)\n\n\nPG: The system to be implemented\nBank: A PG can integrate with multiple banks; candidates may implement mock or full-fledged bank classes\nFeature Requirements\n -> The PG should support onboarding multiple clients\n -> The PG should support multiple bank integrations (e.g., HDFC, ICICI, SBI)\n -> The PG should support multiple payment methods such as UPI, Credit/Debit Card, and Net Banking\n -> The PG should route payments to specific banks based on defined criteria\n    -> Example: credit card payments go to HDFC, \n    -> net banking to ICICI\n-> The PG should support traffic distribution across banks\n    -> Example: 30% traffic to Bank A and 70% to Bank B\n-> Clients should be able to choose supported payment methods\n    -> Example: only UPI, or all except net banking\n\n\nAssumptions\n-> Banks may randomly return success or failure; this can be mocked using a random function\n-> Payments should be processed only if required parameters are provided\n    -> Net banking requires user ID and password\n    -> Card payments require card details\n    -> Banks require OTP verification after validating instrument details\n    -> For simplicity, OTP verification can be skipped\n\n\n\nCode Expectations\n-> All data should be maintained in memory\n-> Any programming language can be used\n-> Simple function-based entry points are expected\n-> No credit for REST APIs or framework-heavy implementations\n-> Emphasis should be on clean design and implementation rather than complex algorithms\n\n\n\nRequired Functions\n-> addClient(): Add a client to the PG -> clientservice -> clientmodel -> clientdao\n-> removeClient(): Remove a client from the PG. -> clientservice  clientdao\n-> hasClient(): Check whether a client exists in the PG -> \n-> listSupportedPaymodes(): Show payment modes supported by the PG\n    -> If a client is passed, show payment modes supported by that client\n-> addSupportForPaymode(): Add payment mode support to the PG\n    -> If a client is passed, add payment mode support for that client\n-> removePaymode(): Remove a payment mode from the PG or a specific client\n-> showDistribution(): Display the current traffic distribution across banks\n-> makePayment(): Process a payment using the provided payment details\n\n\n\nEvaluation Criteria\n\n-> Working implementation\n-> Code readability and clarity\n-> Proper use of OOP and Object-Oriented Design principles\n-> Clear separation of concerns\n-> Testability and TDD-oriented design\n-> Language proficiency\n-> Test cases (bonus points)\n\n\n\nExtension Problems\n-> Ability for the router to dynamically shift traffic based on bank success rates\n-> Ability to route traffic based on payment instruments, not just banks\n\n\n\nExecution Time Limit: 3 seconds (Java)",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
